  "stem": "The equation \\(3(y - 2) = 4x + 1\\) is graphed in the \\(xy\\)-plane. What is the \\(y\\)-coordinate of the \\(y\\)-intercept of the graph?",
  "figure": null,
  "answer_type": "mcq",
  "choices": {
    "A": "\\(2\\)",
    "B": "\\(7\\)",
    "C": "\\(-\\frac{7}{3}\\)",
    "D": "\\(\\frac{7}{3}\\)"
  },
  "answer": "D",
  "spr_answers": null,
  "solution": [
    "Set \\(x = 0\\): \\(3(y - 2) = 1\\).",
    "Then \\(3y - 6 = 1\\), \\(3y = 7\\), and \\(y = \\frac{7}{3}\\)."
  ],
  "explanation": {
    "A": "Incorrect. 2 is the number subtracted from \\(y\\), not the value of \\(y\\) when \\(x = 0\\).",
    "B": "Incorrect. \\(3y = 7\\) still needs to be divided by 3.",
    "C": "Incorrect. \\(3y = 4x + 7\\) gives a positive \\(y\\)-intercept.",
    "D": "Correct. At \\(x = 0\\): \\(3(y - 2) = 1\\), so \\(y - 2 = \\frac{1}{3}\\) and \\(y = \\frac{7}{3}\\)."
  },
